 \subsection{Macro-ratio identities}\label{si:ratios}
 
 Set $R_B=E/B$, $R_A=E/Y_f$, and $\psi_f=Y_f/B$. Then $R_A=R_B/\psi_f$ by definition. The direct-yield share of capital contribution, $\psi_c=b_cA_c/Y_c$, is a different ratio; $R_A=R_B/\psi_c$ is not an identity. Aggregate balance occurs at $R_B=1$. With baseline shares $\sigma_f=\sigma_c=1$, $R_A>1$ is necessary but not sufficient for conversion: shortfall requires $E>B=Y_f+Y_c$, not merely $E>Y_f$. The threshold changes when flows are reserved. Capital-land area falls when $u_c>R_{rc}$, with $u_c$ defined in Eq.~(5). Thus, under baseline shares, conversion requires $R_B>1$. Under the reservation extension with $\sigma_f,\sigma_c<1$, conversion and declining $A_c$ can occur even when $R_B\le1$; aggregate overshoot is not required. At $q^*=q_{\max}/2$, the direct-yield share of capital value is $b_c/(b_c+b_{G,c}\rho_cq_{\max}/4)=0.758$ at baseline. This value applies at that point; it is not a structural constant.
 \section{Numerical methods and sensitivity analyses}\label{si:numerics}
 
-\paragraph{Numerical method.} We integrate the delay equations by the method of steps with an explicit first-order Euler scheme. The positive-part operator is exact. We enforce $A_c\ge\Acmin$, $A_f\ge0$, $q\in[0,q_{\max}]$, $P,D\ge0$, and $K\ge K_{\min}$, and calculate reserve area as $A_r=A_{tot}-A_f-A_c$. Histories of $B$ and $q$ remain at their initial values over $[-\max(\tau_g,\tau_p),0)$. In the technology-wave experiment, both runs share the same no-wave history. The active $T_b(0)$ changes current $B(0)$, and later wave-adjusted values enter the delayed input $K(t-\tau_p)$. We use $\Delta t=0.05$\,yr for the demonstration and threshold bisection, and $0.1$\,yr for drawdown sweeps; selected thresholds are checked at $0.05$\,yr. At $A_{c0}=1.0$\,ha, the $10^{-6}$\,ha floor-proximity value shifts from $2.955$ to $2.98$\,cap across these step sizes. We therefore report thresholds as bands that depend on tolerance and numerical resolution.
-
-\paragraph{Delayed versus current quality in the regeneration term.} All reported trajectories use the delayed law $G_c(q(t-\tau_g))$ in Eq.~(10). We also test the current-quality alternative, $G_c(q(t))$. Because $q$ is monotone and regeneration contributes at most $0.016$\,gha\,ha$^{-1}$—compared with direct yield $b_c=0.05$\,gha\,ha$^{-1}$—the summary differences stay below $1\%$. The current-quality version extends the demonstration window by $1.3$\,yr and shifts the initial-population floor-proximity value by at most $0.001$\,cap (Table~\ref{tab:si_delay}).
-
